SUMMARY:Autumn Awe at Crawford Lake
DESCRIPTION:Wilderness Union Toronto - Outdoor Adventure and Hiking Club\n
 **Please note the only way to register for this adventure is on [our websi
 te.](https://www.wildernessunion.com/adventures/crawford-lake-26)**\n**Cra
 wford Lake keeps its secrets in its sediment. We’ll share some of them w
 ith you on this adventure.**\nThis emerald green meromictic lake is a scie
 ntific marvel. It’s so still that its deep layers never mix\, and nothin
 g that settles to the bottom ever gets disturbed. That untouched geologica
 l record\, laid down year by year\, is part of why it’s so special. We
 ’ll follow the lake’s shores on the newly completed boardwalk\, with r
 adiant fall colours towering above us.\nFrom the lake\, the trail climbs o
 nto the Niagara Escarpment and joins the Bruce Trail\, the longest trail n
 etwork in Ontario. This section has some moderately technical terrain that
  will ask you to slow down and pay attention.\nPartway through\, the trail
  passes the reconstructed 15th century Iroquoian village\, three longhouse
 s rebuilt on their original footprint.\nFrom there\, the trail carries on 
 toward Rattlesnake Point\, climbing to a pretty spectacular viewpoint over
  the canyon before looping back through mixed hardwood forest to the lake.
  By the time you're back at the boardwalk\, you'll have covered the escarp
 ment's best angles: canyon\, cliff\, and calm water\, all in one loop.\n**
 Accessibility**\nWilderness Union is committed to making our adventures as
  accessible as possible. If you wish to make an accommodation request rela
 ting to a disability or provide feedback\, please do so by sending an emai
 l to info@wildernessunion.com\n**Highlights**\n**Rattlesnake Point Viewpoi
 nt:** The high point of the loop\, with open views out over the escarpment
  cliffs and the valley below.\n**Crawford Lake:** Formed roughly 10\,000 y
 ears ago in a sinkhole carved out by retreating glaciers\, tucked into for
 est at the top of the escarpment.\n**Peak Fall Colours:** The escarpment f
 orest turns early and hard here\, with the ridgeline views making the reds
  and golds even more dramatic than from the trail floor.\n**Itinerary**\n*
 *09:00** \\- Shuttle leaves from near Keele station\n**10:00 - 10:30** \\-
  Explore Iroquoian Longhouse Village\n**10:30 - 15:30** \\- Hike Crawford 
 lake loop and to Rattlesnake point\\\, with a stop for lunch along the way
 \n**16:30** \\- Arrival back in Toronto\n**Event cost: $55+HST. $40 option
 al transport also available. Please register [here.](https://www.wildernes
 sunion.com/adventures/crawford-lake-26)**\n**About Wilderness Union**\nWil
 derness Union is deliberate about building an outdoors community that conn
 ects you to the life-changing magic of the outdoors and helps you find you
 r people.\nMembers range from 20-40 years old. The group is a mix of GTA l
 ocals and newcomers to Toronto from within Canada and abroad.\nAttendance 
 numbers on Meetup don't reflect our actual event attendance - we typically
  have 15-20 community members at each event. Large enough that there's som
 eone new to meet but small enough that it's not overwhelming!\n***Learn mo
 re at our website [www.wildernessunion.com](https://www.wildernessunion.co
 m/)***
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/wildernessunion/events/316861703/
